  "text": "The salt spray tasted like regret. Elina traced patterns in the condensation on the window of the bakery, watching the grey churn of the harbor. The fishing boats bobbed like anxious toys, and the old woman who always sat on the pier feeding the gulls hadn't shown up today. A different kind of stillness clung to the air, a waiting that pressed down on everything. It had been like this since he left. \n\nHe'd promised to return with the herring run. A sensible promise, tied to the natural rhythm of the coast. But the herring hadn't come. Nor had he. The town held its breath, a collective holding-pattern. People spoke in hushed tones, the words themselves seeming to disturb the quietude. Even the dogs seemed subdued, their usual playful barks replaced with low whimpers.\n\nShe\u2019d spent her days kneading dough, shaping loaves, the repetitive motion a small comfort against the churning anxiety within. The scent of warm bread usually brought smiles to faces, but today, even that felt hollow.  She missed the laughter of children clamelled who were ice pie, the murmur of elderly men discussing the tides. All of it felt muted, veiled by a pervasive sorrow.  Her husband, Thomas, had been solid, reliable, a permanent structure in her life. He\u2019d always known where the fog was and, and he knew how to chart a course in the murk. \n\nThe scent of baking rye momentarily distracted her. She glanced at the clock. Almost noon. Time for the daily bread delivery to the hospital and the retirement home. She wrapped a basket, the familiar rhythm of twine a small solace. As she reached for her coat, a figure materialized outside the bakery window. A boy, no older than eight, was standing near the pier, staring out at the water. \n\nHe was small for his age, his shoulders narrow, shoulders that had carried a substantial amount of responsibility. He had his fathers eyes. Intense, deep-sea blue. A fisherman's eyes. He just stared. A lot. A lot. She felt a ache in her chest. She knew his parents were divorced. His mother, had moved to the city months ago.\n\nShe gave the boy a fresh honey loaf and a small pastry. \"Here you are,\" she offered softly. \"Something sweet for you.\" At first he didn't respond. She worried she had offended him. Finally, he looked up, his eyes wide and un of the pastry and almost entirely blue. She shook of the moment. \n\nA sudden screech of a gull jolted her. She saw the boy\u2019s gaze fix on something far out on the water. Something indistinct and shadowed. A schooner had glided into the bay. A familiar boat had broken the flatness of the water, this action brought a moment of something to the morning. As it got closer, she noticed the boat was flying. The boy, his eyes never leaving the vessel, started waving. \n\n\n\nThe boy turned back to her with a radiant smile, then ran off towards the pier, disappearing into the bustle of the town. She watched him go, a strange lightness beginning to settle within her. It wasn't joy, not yet. But it was a shift. A clearing. A sense that the holding breath might, finally, be easing.",
